1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,840
accounting because we were forgetting

2
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:04,280
that the workers were overpaid.

3
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:06,200
And then we're not paying a billion

4
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,360
to go transfer $0.6 billion.

5
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:11,680
We're paying a billion to transfer $1.6 billion.

6
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:14,760
And all we need to do is admit that the workers are actually

7
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:16,720
totally, grossly, grossly overpaid

8
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:18,480
relative to the qualifications.

9
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:23,200
And then we got a total cost-benefit analysis.

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,000
This was, I think, Bill Clinton's first CEA head

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,640
was Lyndon Lawrence Tyson.

12
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:31,320
And she actually did cost-benefit analysis

13
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:33,040
like this, arguing for investor policy.

14
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,380
But again, it's one that never got much public attention.

15
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:39,080
Because normally, when you want to defend anything

16
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:41,600
that you're subsidizing, do you say that the workers

17
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:43,480
are grossly overpaid?

18
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,040
No.

19
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,120
What you say is, they earn every penny.

20
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:48,620
But if they're earning every penny,

21
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:51,640
then counter-excess is a terrible idea.

22
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:54,440
In order to make it a good idea, you just say, no.

23
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:55,680
I mean, come on.

24
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:57,180
They're totally unskilled workers

25
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:59,080
and they're making $50 an hour.

26
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:02,640
So you've got to remember that before you pull the plug on it.

27
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:06,160
It's very important.

28
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:08,080
Another analogous case of this is,

29
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:10,160
when people are doing cost-benefit analysis,

30
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:12,560
they will occasionally do something really crummy,

31
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:13,800
which is say, well, look.

32
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,360
This is going to create 100 jobs.

33
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:18,840
And those workers are going to get, say, $10 million.

34
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:22,720
And so that $10 million is a transfer to those workers.

35
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:25,040
Is the $10 million that you give to those workers,

36
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:29,000
like their salary is $10 million, is that really,

37
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,200
economically speaking, a transfer of $10 million?

38
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:31,700
No.

39
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:32,360
No, why not?

40
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:33,520
Because the workers have an opportunity cost.

41
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:35,200
Yeah, they have an opportunity cost.

42
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:36,900
So the workers, the next best alternative

43
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,760
is actually to make $10 million.

44
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:43,360
Then you actually haven't transferred anything to them.

45
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:45,320
And if you're overpaying them by 5%,

46
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:47,960
then there's a 5% transfer to them.

47
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:49,560
But it's much less.

48
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:54,080
So any time someone talks about, we

49
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:57,480
have to think about all the benefits of all the workers,

50
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:59,400
the benefits is not the salary.

51
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:01,560
The benefit is the difference between the salary

52
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:05,440
and the opportunity cost, which, again,

53
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:06,680
goes back to the same point.

54
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:08,180
If their opportunity cost is nothing

55
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:10,760
because they're incompetent, then, yeah,

56
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:13,920
then it's right to count it that way.

57
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:15,480
So we know very often when economists

58
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:18,240
talk about the distributional effects of deregulation,

59
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:20,160
a lot of them say, well, a whole lot of lawyers

60
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:20,800
are going to lose their jobs.

61
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:23,000
And then they sometimes even put in the whole salary

62
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:23,880
of the lawyer.

63
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:25,580
It's like, well, I think these salaries,

64
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:27,520
there's nothing else on Earth they can do.

65
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:29,920
For obviously, most of them are going to just go and become

66
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:31,920
a different kind of lawyer.

67
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:34,200
But suppose that there were a massive reduction

68
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:35,240
in the number of lawyers.

69
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:38,360
Are those guys just going to go and become beggars?

70
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:39,320
They'll find something.

71
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:42,960
Almost all of them will find something else to do.

72
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:46,360
So a key implication of cost-benefit analysis,

73
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:49,440
which is very rarely understood, even by people doing it,

74
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:51,560
and again, why is this counterintuitive to think

75
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:54,200
of the way that you want to defend something in cost-benefit

76
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:56,840
terms and say the workers are overpaid?

77
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:59,000
Doesn't sound like a normal argument.

78
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,640
It's just a sound argument, or at least it's a valid argument.

79
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:05,040
It's one that is consistent with the system.

80
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:07,760
Whereas if you say the workers deserve every penny they get,

81
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:09,600
that's when you say, oh, well, in that case,

82
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,760
let's not pay them.

83
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:14,080
It only makes sense to pay them if they don't deserve

84
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:15,600
most of what they're getting.

85
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,360
Then it passes the cost-benefit test, otherwise not.

86
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,440
Other questions?

87
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:23,840
OK, are anyone happy?

88
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:25,040
Anyone unhappy?

89
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:27,680
All right, now let's move into probability,

90
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:29,600
objective and subjective.

91
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:34,680
So probability language lets us quantify uncertainty.

92
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:36,720
So there's more to say in an uncertain world

93
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:39,640
than I don't know.

94
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:40,720
You could answer that way.

95
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:42,480
So what are the odds that you get struck by lightning?

96
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:43,320
I don't know.

97
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:44,880
What are the odds that Trump will still be president tomorrow?

98
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:45,880
I don't know.

99
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:48,040
What are the odds that you eat dinner tonight?

100
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:49,080
I don't know that either.

101
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:50,640
I don't know anything.

102
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:53,480
So since it's not certain, it's not impossible,

103
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:55,760
I just plead the fifth.

104
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:57,240
You can do that.

105
00:03:57,240 --> 00:04:00,520
But there's a lot more that you can say in an uncertain world.

106
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:04,600
Now there's a couple different versions of probability theory.

107
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:07,800
So the first one, which is controversial with almost no

108
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:12,480
one, is called objective probability theory.

109
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:13,880
Objective probability theory.

110
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:19,000
Objective probability theory.

111
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:19,640
All right.

112
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:23,840
So this just says that even when you don't know what will happen,

113
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:26,560
you're still allowed to talk about the relative frequencies

114
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:29,600
of observed events in the past.

115
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:31,920
So I don't know what's going to happen,

116
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:35,320
but what I can say is that Democrats

117
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:38,960
have won the popular vote in four of the last six elections.

118
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:41,920
I think it might be now five of the last six.

119
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:42,480
All right.

120
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:44,600
So you can say, so the probability,

121
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,440
the objective probability that Democrats

122
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:51,680
are going to win the popular vote will be five of six, say.

123
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:54,280
Or of course, you do this for actuarial tables.

124
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:57,920
Are you going to live to be 70?

125
00:04:57,920 --> 00:04:58,800
I don't know.

126
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,640
But what I can say is that the fraction

127
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:04,560
of people in your demographic who make it to 70 in the past

128
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:05,800
has been this.

129
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:08,320
And that would be the objective probability.

130
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:13,080
Or you could do this with dice or with cards.

131
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:14,920
In all these cases, you can say, look,

132
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:17,240
there's an objective probability.

133
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:21,640
So in principle, you'd have to actually roll the dice

134
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:22,160
empirically.

135
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:25,840
Then say empirically, the six-sided die

136
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:28,280
comes out one 1-6th of the time, two 1-6th of the time,

137
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:29,560
three 1-6th of the time.

138
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:31,280
That's the frequency in the past.

139
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,680
And so there's a 1-6th chance that if we throw it again,

140
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:34,480
it will be a three.

141
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:40,840
So virtually no one says there's any philosophical problem

142
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:42,760
with this concept.

143
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:44,200
Anyone here?

144
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:46,480
No, you can't talk about what happened in the past.

145
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:48,200
Assumes there is a past.

146
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:50,880
Assumes that something happens.

147
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:52,680
There are weird philosophical views

148
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:55,360
where there's someone who rejects everything.

149
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:59,520
So you can't please everybody.

150
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:01,600
Now, objective probability theory, unconversial,

151
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:05,480
but it's not very satisfying when you think about it.

152
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:07,240
For one thing, objective probability theory

153
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:09,440
implies that you cannot talk about the probability

154
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:11,560
of any unique event.

155
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:14,520
Because how often do you observe a unique event?

156
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:16,220
For any unique event you're talking about

157
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:18,720
hasn't happened yet, how many times have you observed it?

158
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:19,320
Zero.

159
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:20,000
Zero.

160
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,320
How many times does it come out any particular way?

161
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:24,360
Zero.

162
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:27,560
What's zero over zero?

163
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:28,560
Undefined.

164
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:29,680
Undefined.

165
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:30,720
So it's indeterminate.

166
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:32,960
I think that additional is indeterminate.

167
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:34,480
It's an indeterminate thing.

168
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:36,440
Like remember taking limits, L'Hospital's rule,

169
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:37,240
that kind of thing.

170
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:41,520
Zero over zero could actually be 1 third.

171
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:43,040
But anyway, so that's the problem.

172
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:45,080
So it says you can't talk about the probability

173
00:06:45,080 --> 00:06:47,400
of any unique event.

174
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:49,320
And that seems weird because don't we often

175
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:51,160
talk about the probability of unique events?

176
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:53,560
What's the probability that you will get a job

177
00:06:53,560 --> 00:06:57,280
at Northwestern University?

178
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:58,600
That's a unique event.

179
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:00,400
So we're not talking about anybody else,

180
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:03,600
we're talking about you, this particular case.

181
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:05,000
And then here's a bigger problem.

182
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,420
You say, well, all right, fine, we lose the unique events,

183
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:09,440
but how many events are unique?

184
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:12,920
Strictly speaking, all events are unique.

185
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:17,240
Even playing roulette is unique because there's only one time

186
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:20,120
that you're there in that casino,

187
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:24,040
on that night, at that time, at that table, playing there,

188
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:26,040
and you don't know that table's fair, do you?

189
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:32,280
So have you run the test on whether the table's fair?

190
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:35,200
So in that case, once again, like Ulysses,

191
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:36,640
unique probability.

192
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:40,360
Then we get lucky number 33, spinning this wheel.

193
00:07:40,360 --> 00:07:44,040
Me, Brian Kaplan, spinning it here at Harris Casino

194
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:48,600
at 11.32 PM this night.

195
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:49,520
That's a unique event.

196
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:52,360
It's never happened before, it will never happen again.

197
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:53,860
And if you think about it, any event

198
00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:55,360
can be described as a unique event

199
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:58,640
because every event is, in fact, a unique event.

200
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:02,800
An actuary may say, you are a 46-year-old male with a PhD

201
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:05,520
living in the 22124 zip code.

202
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:07,440
That's what you are.

203
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:09,480
I say, well, I'm also, like, yes,

204
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:11,520
but what about the table for people

205
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:13,120
who play role-playing games?

206
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:15,760
What about the table for people who also own a pool table?

207
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:18,480
What about, like, well, there isn't anyone else in that

208
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:20,240
who fits that description.

209
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:24,240
So you can't say, well, my odds are dying.

210
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:27,240
So, and I'm like, oh, yes, we can.

211
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:28,800
We're going to do it anyway.

212
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:30,240
I don't care what philosophers say.

213
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:33,040
We're going to give up probability in any case.

214
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:34,080
All right.

215
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:37,040
Now, when I had this argument again with Walter Bloch

216
00:08:37,040 --> 00:08:39,800
and pointed out that since everything is unique,

217
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:41,680
he said, well, object to probability is fine.

218
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:43,800
I said, yeah, but in the real world, everything's unique.

219
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:45,200
And then he said, great.

220
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:46,240
Then we can never use it.

221
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:47,920
Even better.

222
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:50,080
We can never talk about the probability of anything.

223
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:51,240
Oh, wonderful.

224
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:52,080
All right.

225
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:56,120
Now, you know, even he does say things

226
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:58,960
that sure sound like probability statements on occasion.

227
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:01,240
And he has a whole world view where, yeah,

228
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:02,760
but I'm not speaking as a scientist.

229
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:05,120
I'm speaking as a person.

230
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:07,720
Well, is the person wrong?

231
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:10,200
And so if the person's wrong, then stop saying it.

232
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:12,160
If the person's right, why not go

233
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:14,280
and use the language that allows us to communicate

234
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:16,280
with other people?

235
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:17,680
I'm good with that.

236
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:20,120
I don't know.

237
00:09:20,120 --> 00:09:23,920
I cannot successfully channel him on this either.

238
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:27,040
So once you realize that object to probability theory

239
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:29,880
leaves us hollow, leaves us without answers

240
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:32,120
for so many questions that we need to answer,

241
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:34,640
brings us readily to subject to probability theory.

242
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:38,960
All right.

243
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:41,560
So subject to probability theory says

244
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:45,040
that a subject to probability is simply a degree of belief.

245
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:46,920
It is a degree of belief that a person

246
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:48,760
assigns to a proposition.

247
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:49,960
And that's it.

248
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:53,920
So you can have a 10% probability, or a 3%, or 99.

249
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:56,560
So all we need is that probability

250
00:09:56,560 --> 00:09:59,560
has to range from, has to be greater than or equal to 0%,

251
00:09:59,560 --> 00:10:02,080
less than or equal to 100%, or greater

252
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:06,480
than or equal to 1%, less than or equal to 1%,

253
00:10:06,480 --> 00:10:11,640
less than or equal to 1%, less than or equal to 0%,

254
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:13,960
greater than or equal to 0%, less than or equal to 1%.

255
00:10:13,960 --> 00:10:14,840
Yes, that's correct.

256
00:10:14,840 --> 00:10:15,880
That's seven other words.

257
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:16,380
Zero.

258
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:21,560
Ignore all the words I said and that.

259
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:23,080
That's good.

260
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:24,040
OK.

261
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:26,920
And normally we say that a probability of 0

262
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:28,320
means impossible.

263
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:31,520
Were there any undergraduate math majors here?

264
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:32,020
Good.

265
00:10:32,020 --> 00:10:34,840
OK, in that case, it's true.

266
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:37,960
So if you remember infinitesimal things and so on,

267
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:40,000
technically speaking, it is logically

268
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:41,960
possible for a zero probability event to occur.

269
00:10:41,960 --> 00:10:44,240
Like, don't worry about it for all practical purposes.

270
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:46,120
That probability of 0 is possible.

271
00:10:46,120 --> 00:10:49,400
And similarly, probability of 1 is certain.

272
00:10:49,400 --> 00:10:52,720
And then the other thing is that since something always happens,

273
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:56,320
have you ever been in a case where nothing happened?

274
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:59,960
It's like, even nothing is something.

275
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:02,840
So then it's also that since it is certain that something

276
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:07,120
or other will occur, then the summation of all probabilities

277
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:08,200
must equal 1.

278
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:10,440
As long as these probabilities are mutually exclusive

279
00:11:10,440 --> 00:11:13,800
and jointly exhaustive, then the probability of everything

280
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:17,880
will sum up that something or other happening has its 1.

281
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:19,880
And of course, this could be probability

282
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:22,080
that I answer the phone, or my son answers the phone,

283
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:23,440
or my daughter answers the phone,

284
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:25,560
or somebody else answers the phone.

285
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:27,240
And if you do it that way, then that's

286
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:28,860
mutually exclusive, jointly exhaustive.

287
00:11:28,860 --> 00:11:30,400
So that must sum to 1.

288
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:32,480
Because actually, no one has to answer the phone.

289
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:34,480
So last one would be or nobody answers the phone.

290
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:35,560
And that will sum to 1.

291
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:37,240
Conditional on the phone being answered,

292
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:38,280
then someone answered it.

293
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:43,400
Now, why do people have a problem

294
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:45,400
with subjective probability?

295
00:11:45,400 --> 00:11:47,200
The main argument against it is just

296
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:48,880
that it's unrealistic to say that people

297
00:11:48,880 --> 00:11:50,800
have subjective probabilities.

298
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:53,120
Because normally, when beings, as opposed

299
00:11:53,120 --> 00:11:56,320
to Vulcans and robots, do not assign probabilities to events.

300
00:11:56,320 --> 00:12:00,760
So most people do not say, there is a 0.3723 probability

301
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:03,840
that the next meal is a hamburger, something like that.

302
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:04,840
Never tell me the odds.

303
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:06,400
So that's the human way of speaking.

304
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:09,680
So in science fiction, usually it

305
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:13,760
will be robots or Vulcans or other inhuman-type creatures

306
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:18,040
that will be very inclined to give precise probabilities.

307
00:12:18,040 --> 00:12:21,640
And then what does a truly human human like Han Solo

308
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:23,920
say when you start talking this way?

309
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:25,000
Never tell me the odds.

310
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:26,560
Never tell me the odds.

311
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:29,760
Believe it or not, there are odds.

312
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:31,480
Science doesn't want to hear them.

313
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:33,680
But there are people who say, never tell me the odds,

314
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:36,240
because you can't talk about odds.

315
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:41,080
So I guess it's unrealistic to say it.

316
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:45,120
Now, my reply to this is that while it

317
00:12:45,120 --> 00:12:49,520
is true that most people don't go around putting

318
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:52,600
little stickers with numbers on every single event,

319
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:55,240
nevertheless, people almost always

320
00:12:55,240 --> 00:12:57,680
have some probabilities in the back of their minds.

321
00:12:57,680 --> 00:12:58,520
So think about this.

322
00:12:58,520 --> 00:13:00,520
Probabilities like willingness to pay.

323
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:02,600
How many people go around and tell you

324
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:06,400
what their willingness to pay for everything is?

325
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:08,800
My willingness to pay for a new mailbox

326
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:11,360
would be $17.33.

327
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:15,840
Willingness to pay for this mailbox to be of the green

328
00:13:15,840 --> 00:13:19,720
huge would be an upcharge of $1.33.

329
00:13:19,720 --> 00:13:20,220
Right?

330
00:13:20,220 --> 00:13:21,800
Again, this is unusual behavior.

331
00:13:21,800 --> 00:13:23,500
And yet, I don't know anyone in economics

332
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:26,120
who has a problem with the constant willingness to pay.

333
00:13:26,120 --> 00:13:29,520
Because, yes, while people may not, generally,

334
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:31,720
do not have a giant book listing their willingness

335
00:13:31,720 --> 00:13:33,760
to pay for everything, nevertheless,

336
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:37,200
there's some willingness to pay in the back of your minds.

337
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:38,920
It is a meaningful question to say, yeah,

338
00:13:38,920 --> 00:13:41,040
but how much would you be willing to pay for this?

339
00:13:41,040 --> 00:13:42,680
Similarly, it is a meaningful question

340
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:46,280
to say, how likely do you think this is?

341
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:47,400
It doesn't seem like that.

342
00:13:47,400 --> 00:13:50,880
So it seems 50% likely, 75%, 32% likely.

343
00:13:50,880 --> 00:13:54,320
These are all reasonable questions to ask someone.

344
00:13:54,320 --> 00:13:56,160
So yeah, so if you think about probabilities

345
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:57,760
being like willingness to pay, I'll

346
00:13:57,760 --> 00:13:59,800
say either you've got to get rid of both of them.

347
00:13:59,800 --> 00:14:02,000
I don't think even Walter Block wants to do that.

348
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:03,240
Or you've got to accept both.

349
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:04,660
So yeah, well, they're both things

350
00:14:04,660 --> 00:14:07,320
where economics does get us to be more explicit about it.

351
00:14:07,320 --> 00:14:09,360
But yeah, if you do hang around with economists,

352
00:14:09,360 --> 00:14:12,080
you do get more questions like, what is your willingness to pay?

353
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:14,320
So I know one economist who's really

354
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:16,720
worried that he's going to get protested one day.

355
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:18,680
And he talks about it all the time.

356
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:20,840
And I'm like, I'm so tired of hearing that.

357
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:23,440
What is your willingness to pay for an insurance policy that

358
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:26,320
absolutely guarantees that you will never be protested ever

359
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:27,600
in your entire life?

360
00:14:27,600 --> 00:14:28,880
He said $100,000.

361
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:32,160
I'm like, man, you're really worried.

362
00:14:35,160 --> 00:14:36,600
It's not a reasonable number.

363
00:14:36,600 --> 00:14:38,000
I'd like to see you assume that.

364
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:42,440
I don't know, $500 maybe for a lifetime insurance policy.

365
00:14:42,440 --> 00:14:43,480
Just look so far.

366
00:14:43,480 --> 00:14:44,800
I mean, what's been cool to me?

367
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:46,960
I don't see why this would stop.

368
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:51,520
And I think you're just getting agitated by scary stories

369
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:53,440
and forgetting that what is the news?

370
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:54,760
It's an inventory of the worst thing that

371
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:55,920
happened on Earth every day.

372
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:58,600
So you shouldn't basically just pay on that.

373
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:02,000
Anyway, so that is a way that economists think,

374
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:03,360
which is helpful.

375
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:06,760
The elite least you had to say, $100,000.

376
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:09,080
So all right, I guess if I were that worried,

377
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:11,200
I might talk about it a lot too.

378
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:13,240
Although when my house lost more than that money,

379
00:15:13,240 --> 00:15:16,600
I didn't think I needed to keep talking about it.

380
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:19,560
So maybe you could play a bunch of fights, but you're done.

381
00:15:19,560 --> 00:15:21,480
So I'm not going to protest here.

382
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:24,880
Anyway, all right.

383
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:28,320
Now, further objection to subjective probabilities

384
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:30,920
is that if you actually ask someone,

385
00:15:30,920 --> 00:15:33,160
what is the probability you're saying to this?

386
00:15:33,160 --> 00:15:38,040
A lot of people will say, I just don't know.

387
00:15:38,040 --> 00:15:42,120
People will stonewall you and not give you a probability.

388
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:45,880
In fact, you may be treated as being rude for actually asking

389
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:47,000
probabilities.

390
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
Someone says, I'm really worried about terrorism.

391
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:50,400
What's the probability you think?

392
00:15:50,400 --> 00:15:52,000
I don't know.

393
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:53,800
I don't know about it, but I shouldn't even

394
00:15:53,800 --> 00:15:55,480
have to think about questions like that.

395
00:15:58,360 --> 00:15:59,520
Now, here's the key thing.

396
00:15:59,520 --> 00:16:01,040
What if you have to guess?

397
00:16:01,040 --> 00:16:04,240
And in real life, you do have to guess.

398
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:06,600
Every time you eat a meal, you are making a guess

399
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:09,600
that meal is not poisonous.

400
00:16:09,600 --> 00:16:11,360
So far, I assume you've guessed correctly.

401
00:16:14,400 --> 00:16:15,600
Lethal poison.

402
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:19,360
I guess I've had food poisoning once or twice in my life.

403
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:22,640
Didn't kill me, obviously, but it's very unpleasant.

404
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:25,240
If I had guessed correctly on those cases,

405
00:16:25,240 --> 00:16:26,640
I would not have eaten that food.

406
00:16:26,640 --> 00:16:28,360
No way.

407
00:16:28,360 --> 00:16:29,960
The day I was doing my job market talk,

408
00:16:29,960 --> 00:16:34,200
Tyler Cowen tried to get me to try raw Peruvian fish.

409
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:38,160
I'm not trying raw Peruvian fish on the day of my job talk.

410
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:38,800
Super slow.

411
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:43,400
No way.

412
00:16:43,400 --> 00:16:49,840
That was my guess, is that 5% chance I'd be sick,

413
00:16:49,840 --> 00:16:50,760
but that was enough.

414
00:16:50,760 --> 00:16:54,480
I did not want to be sick on the fourth night.

415
00:16:54,480 --> 00:16:56,200
So in real life, you actually do.

416
00:16:56,200 --> 00:16:58,440
You remember when I had my kids, and I always

417
00:16:58,440 --> 00:17:00,600
wanted to say, wow, not only when I cross the street

418
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:03,840
am I now gambling that I can get across the street alive myself.

419
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:06,560
I've got about three lives that I can get across the street

420
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:08,440
safely.

421
00:17:08,440 --> 00:17:11,040
Look to the left, to the right, and left again.

422
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:13,040
Let's go.

423
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:15,440
What if there was a car driving 200 miles per hour?

424
00:17:15,440 --> 00:17:17,840
You wouldn't be able to, like, you'd still die.

425
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:20,320
Yeah, but I've never seen that happen on a city street,

426
00:17:20,320 --> 00:17:23,160
so it seems very unlikely.

427
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:25,600
Could happen.

428
00:17:25,600 --> 00:17:27,880
Now, a common sophism that is associated

429
00:17:27,880 --> 00:17:30,520
with the objection to subjective probability theory

430
00:17:30,520 --> 00:17:32,840
is say, look, no one can ever know that.

431
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:35,120
There's just questions that are unanswerable,

432
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:37,320
that are beyond human comprehension,

433
00:17:37,320 --> 00:17:39,760
that it's arrogant even to have an opinion on.

434
00:17:39,760 --> 00:17:41,160
Like, I remember when I was a kid,

435
00:17:41,160 --> 00:17:42,680
my friend made fun of me for saying,

436
00:17:42,680 --> 00:17:44,760
there have to be exoplanets.

437
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:47,120
This cannot be the only solar system with planets in it.

438
00:17:47,120 --> 00:17:48,160
There's got to be others.

439
00:17:48,160 --> 00:17:49,240
I bet they're everywhere.

440
00:17:49,240 --> 00:17:51,240
And he said, you can't know that, Brian.

441
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:53,280
You can't possibly say one way or the other.

442
00:17:53,280 --> 00:17:54,280
Ha.

443
00:17:54,280 --> 00:17:55,000
All right.

444
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,880
And well, what if we had made a bet?

445
00:17:57,880 --> 00:17:59,480
And yes, if we had made the bets,

446
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:01,120
I would have bet, I think I would have bet a lot on that,

447
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:03,280
and I would have totally been vindicated by science.

448
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:06,320
And now there's thousands of identified exoplanets.

449
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:07,120
Great.

450
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:09,880
I love reading about exoplanets.

451
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:11,480
No interest in subatomic particles,

452
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:14,320
but any planet will always interest me.

453
00:18:14,320 --> 00:18:15,920
Well, that's a place I could colonize.

454
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:18,200
I could never live on a fork.

455
00:18:18,200 --> 00:18:20,280
But good in what sense?

456
00:18:20,280 --> 00:18:21,800
Don't worry about that.

457
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:24,680
I can imagine living on the planet.

458
00:18:24,680 --> 00:18:28,720
Anyway, common selfism is to say no one can possibly know x.

459
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:30,560
There's two ways that we can interpret this.

460
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:32,560
One is, if you mean no one can know x

461
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:34,680
with absolute certainty, then that's totally true,

462
00:18:34,680 --> 00:18:36,920
but totally uninteresting.

463
00:18:36,920 --> 00:18:38,400
Yes, I can never know with certainty

464
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:42,720
that I will not die from eating a bowl of Cheerios.

465
00:18:42,720 --> 00:18:43,840
So what?

466
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:46,360
I'm still perfectly happy to bet my life on these Cheerios.

467
00:18:46,360 --> 00:18:47,680
They seem fine.

468
00:18:47,680 --> 00:18:49,960
So looks good for me.

469
00:18:49,960 --> 00:18:51,320
OK.

470
00:18:51,320 --> 00:18:53,240
So you mean no one can know it with certainty,

471
00:18:53,240 --> 00:18:55,200
then it's obvious, but uninteresting.

472
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:56,840
On the other hand, if you mean no one

473
00:18:56,840 --> 00:19:01,040
can know anything at all about x, then it's clearly false.

474
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:02,920
And how can you know it's so clearly false?

475
00:19:02,920 --> 00:19:03,920
Well, how about this?

476
00:19:03,920 --> 00:19:06,560
If you really think no one can know about it at all,

477
00:19:06,560 --> 00:19:08,040
how about we just make a bet?

478
00:19:08,040 --> 00:19:10,440
So what does that mean?

479
00:19:10,440 --> 00:19:11,560
50-50?

480
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:15,160
Fine, we'll give you 50-50 on a Martian invasion tomorrow.

481
00:19:15,160 --> 00:19:17,080
I'll give you three to one even.

482
00:19:17,080 --> 00:19:18,560
How about that?

483
00:19:18,560 --> 00:19:24,200
So when you all say, so I don't know if you've heard of that.

484
00:19:24,200 --> 00:19:26,560
So one of my main activities on the internet

485
00:19:26,560 --> 00:19:28,000
is just betting people that I think

486
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,440
are being giant blowhards and who claim

487
00:19:30,440 --> 00:19:32,160
to know way more than they do.

488
00:19:32,160 --> 00:19:32,800
So let's see.

489
00:19:32,800 --> 00:19:35,560
My current record is 17 and 0 for my public bets.

490
00:19:35,560 --> 00:19:38,160
So I've won 100% so far.

491
00:19:38,160 --> 00:19:40,200
There's two more that I'm pretty much guaranteed

492
00:19:40,200 --> 00:19:42,640
to win once the data come out this month.

493
00:19:42,640 --> 00:19:45,440
So it'll be 19 and 0.

494
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:48,920
So of course, I could screw up and start messing up.

495
00:19:48,920 --> 00:19:50,760
And I don't want to get cocky about this.

496
00:19:50,760 --> 00:19:53,760
But anyway, so when someone makes a big claim

497
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:56,680
about what's going to happen, if you really think that,

498
00:19:56,680 --> 00:19:59,400
then it seems like you should be open to a bet.

499
00:19:59,400 --> 00:20:00,880
If you really think that, then you


